\documentclass{article}

\title{Answers at the back of The Book}
\subtitle{Why solved math is still fun.}
\date{2026-10-27}
\modified{2026-10-27}

\keyword{ai}
\keyword{math}

\begin{document}

Sometime during my second year at university,
learning math stopped being drudgery and started to feel like visiting fantastic places.
I wandered the bonsai gardens of abstract algebra,
peered into the abysses of transfinite numbers,
and sailed the chaotic oceans of dynamical systems.
I would never have reached these places without guides:
textbooks, teachers, and fellow students.
I climbed staircases polished by the feet of those who came before me.

Math is one of my biggest joys.
And according to recent headlines, it’s in trouble.

In August 2026,
Daniel Litt warned that OpenAI’s progress might mean
\href{https://www.daniellitt.com/blog/2026/8/11/the-end-of-mathematics/}{the end of mathematics}.
On September 8th,
OpenAI published
\href{https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/}{a solution to the Navier-Stokes Millennium Prize Problem}.
On October 6th,
the company released
\href{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/301488868beec11bfd897168433b0a64f5258559/overview.pdf}{over 700 manuscripts} with new mathematical results.

The response of the math community was mixed.
Litt outlined a new \href{https://www.daniellitt.com/blog/2026/9/13/a-beginning-for-mathematics/}{beginning for mathematics}.
Tristan Buckmaster,
who used \textsc{llm}s to prove the \href{https://cims.nyu.edu/~tristanb/euler.pdf}{blowup of Euler's equations},
\href{https://cims.nyu.edu/~tristanb/statement.pdf}{called} his experience a Deep Blue-Kasparov moment.
Terence Tao,
an early advocate of formalization and \textsc{ai},
published a declaration proclaiming
\href{https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/09/11/a-severe-misalignment-of-ai-in-mathematics/}{A Severe Misalignment of \textsc{ai} in Mathematics}
signed by 25 Fields Medalists.

Mathematicians are worried that \textsc{ai} produces answers
that don't yield better understanding.
Though I agree with this sentiment,
I don't think \textsc{ai} takes away the joy.
Transforming answers into understanding is what people studying math do.
Why should it matter whether answers come from the back of a textbook or an \textsc{llm}?

Almost all math that humans engage with is solved math.
School kids memorize theorems known for millennia.
Undergraduates study centuries-old theories.
PhD students absorb decades of research before they can meaningfully contribute.
International Mathematical Olympiad participants deal with problems
that have known solutions.

Professional mathematicians love inventing neater proofs of old theorems.
Early in his career,
Peter Scholze distilled a
\href{https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691090924/the-geometry-and-cohomology-of-some-simple-shimura-varieties}{288-page proof by Michael Harris and Richard Taylor}
to \href{https://people.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/scholze/LocalLanglands.pdf}{37 pages}.
The \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Four_color_theorem}{four-color theorem} is settled,
yet mathematicians won't leave it alone
until they have a proof that doesn't involve computers\sidenote{sn-4-color}{
``What I would like is a proof without the use of a computer,''
Thomassen said.
``And I will never stop thinking about that.''
\newline
--- \bibref{quanta-four-color-new-proof}}.
Casting old math into new shapes gives new insights,
as witnessed by
\href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolas_Bourbaki}{Bourbaki},
\href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Constructivism_(philosophy_of_mathematics)}{constructivists},
\href{https://classes.golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2014/01/an_elementary_theory_of_the_ca.html}{category theorists},
and, most recently, \href{https://xenaproject.wordpress.com}{formalization aficionados}.

Knowing that the answer exists is a great motivator.
I like to imagine that Fermat's remark about his Last Theorem,
``I have discovered a truly marvelous proof of this,
which this margin is too narrow to contain,''
was a clever hoax.
It created an illusion that a proof was within reach,
pushing generations of mathematicians to try harder.
Conversely, progress on the quasi-Riemann Hypothesis stalled for a long time
because mathematicians believed it required novel ideas.
\textsc{ai} proved them wrong using their own tools\sidenote{sn-quanta-quasi-riemann}{
``contrary to what mathematicians had long expected,
the proof didn't need to introduce any new concepts—it instead unearthed hidden connections between old ones''
\newline
--- \bibref{quanta-quasi-riemann}}.

Mathematicians stay relevant even in a world
where \textsc{ai} can conjure perfect proofs straight from \href{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proofs_from_THE_BOOK}{The Book}.
Mathematics feels like an eternal space independent of human existence,
but it comes to life in the minds of people,
when a teacher makes a student want to explore it.
People care about things that other people care about.

The wonders of math don't become less beautiful or enjoyable
just because a silicon mind found them first and sent you a selfie.
You still have to climb there yourself to claim them, ideally in good company.
There will never be a royal road.
\end{document}
